A||||12||Fiero_24||2.8 to 3.1?||||5||
Z||000000||Fiero_24||07-10-2001||08:31 AM||||I am going to build a new 2.8 from my Fiero with all new parts.  Sould I use the 3.1 stroker kit from the Fiero store?  I planned on adding headers, bigger intake, performance cam, and whatever else I can to up the HP.  <P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||07-10-2001||03:59 PM||||The Fiero store doesn't have a stroker kit.<BR>Are you talking about their 2.8 performance kit with the forged TRW pistons?<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||07-10-2001||05:01 PM||||Id just rebuild a 3.1 to start with. cost the same more or less. then your sure u got the right stuff. The crank is the most important part of the stroker kits. It dont break like the 2.8 does.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_24||07-10-2001||05:27 PM||||Sorry about that, I thought it was the 3.1 kit that The Fiero Store sold.  Is the 2.8 performance kit good to go with that they sell?  I want power, but I do not want to spend too much.  I am doing the motor a little at a time so I can afford a little here and there, but I want to know what to get ahead of time.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||07-10-2001||07:05 PM||||The Fiero Store performance kit is good in that it is convenient but the price is very steep. I bet you could get all those parts cheaper if you bought them separately from a place like Northern Auto Parts. <P>Spend your money on a very good machinist, have the head runners cleaned up/ported, don't make the runners bigger just clean up the casting flash and smooth out the bowl area a little and fit them with SI stainless steel valves. Have the machinist do a performance valve job with cutters not stones and have them back cut the valves for better low lift flow. Get a Crane PowerMax H-260-2 camshaft with the matching valve springs, lifters, and retainers. Get Competition Cams magnum roller tip rockers and new pushrods. Port your old manifolds or get a set of headers like FOCOA headers. Focus on engine breathing! Dull any sharp edges in your combustion chambers with a file or they may cause detonation.<P>For the bottom end buy a new damper if the rubber on the old one is crack, even a little. Have the machinist balance the flywheel/flex plate, damper, crankshaft, connecting rods, and pistons.  Have the connecting rods rebuilt and fitted them with ARP connecting rod bolts (important). Index the damper to TDC and mark the damper so that you know the cut in the damper is really TDC, most are not. You can get the TRW forged pistons anywhere, but for a street machine hypereutectic pistons are a better choice. Hypereutectic pistons cost less, are more than strong enough for a street engine, run with tighter piston to bore clearances, and make more power than forged pistons. You may not be able to get then in a 9.5-1 compression ratio. If you cant than get the Forged pistons.  Get a good set of molly rings and stay away from those gimmicky gapless rings. Get a high volume oil pump, not a cheap one. Have the block decked square to each other and parallel to the main. When the machinist decks the block try to deck it so that the piston to head (quench area) is about .040” for better anti-knock tolerance but no more than .060”. If you do this you will also have to plane down the bottom intake manifold. If the crankshaft needs to be reground than have the machine shop correct any of the journals that do not have the correct 2.99” stroke. Use Clevite engine bearings. <P>Get either a Felpro or ROL master gasket set and make sure it has the rubber valve cover gaskets not the cork one’s. Most importantly keep everything super clean. <P>I recommend you read the this book.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Img_0503_1.jpg"> <BR>It's got all the info you need to know about building a high performance 60-degree v6.   <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||07-10-2001||07:08 PM||||O-yeah If you have a manual transmission than the Fiero Shop sells an aluminum flywheel that weighs half the weight of the original one. It will really help you power out of corners.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||07-10-2001||11:15 PM||||I agree with almost everything said above but<BR>I don't think that hyper. pistons make more power than forged and the cam should be chosen with your driving habits in mind.<BR>Comp. Cams makes a good cam for the 2.8,3.1,<BR>3.4 too. <P>As a side note i'd just like to say that there's nothing like some forged pistons to give you that warm fuzzy feeling of security.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||4
